## 📄 Brief

Change the `Utf8JsonWriter` to always insert newlines **between** the **end of comments** and the **start of arrays or objects** when `JsonWriterOptions.Indented` is set to `true`.

This change will produce Json that is more human-readable and in-line with Json that is hand-authored.

I would go so far as to say that comments at the start of a document should **always** be on their own lines.

Below are differences between the actual result (highlighted in **red**) and the proposed result (highlighted in **green**).

```diff
- /*This is a comment at the top of the document.*/{
+ /*This is a comment at the top of the document.*/
+ {
    "SimpleProperty": "String Value"
  }
```

```diff
  {
    "ObjectProperty":
-   /*This is a comment before an object value.*/{
+   /*This is a comment before an object value.*/
+   {
      "ChildProperty": "Property Value"
    }
  }
```

```diff
- /*This is a comment at the top of the document.*/[
+ /*This is a comment at the top of the document.*/
+ [
    /*This is a comment before an array element.*/
    [
      /*This is a comment before an object in an array.*/
      {
        "ChildProperty":
-       /*This is a comment before an array value.*/[
+       /*This is a comment before an array value.*/
+       [
          {
            "ChildProperty": "Property Value"
          }
        ]
      }
    ]
  ]
```

This change is a **breaking change**, but does so to better serve the original intent behind the `JsonWriterOptions.Indented` functionality (to produce output that is human-readable).

> "There are **other issues** with writing comments using the `Utf8JsonWriter`; this ticket does not intend to address all of the other issues, but instead proposes an **incremental improvement** over the current behaviour."

## 🔎 Detailed Description

Always inserting a newline before the start of objects and arrays is a common way of formatting comments in Json-like serialized data.

Deciding how Json-like data should be formatted is a controversial topic; the topic of how comments should be included in the mix is even more controversial.

> ⚠ Currently, you are unable to write comments **after** commas in objects (as comments are always placed after the previous value in a property).
>
> For this to be fixed there will have to be API changes made to facilitate both writing **before and after** commas that are delimiting properties in Json.

### Adding newlines between the end of comments and the start of objects

#### ✅ Test Case

```csharp
using System;
using System.IO;
using System.Text.Json;

namespace SystemTextJsonCommentNewlineProposal.Objects
{
    class Program
    {
        private static void Main(string[] args)
        {
            var output = new MemoryStream();
            var writer = new Utf8JsonWriter(output, new JsonWriterOptions()
            {
                // Important as proposed changes should only occur to indented Json only.
                Indented = true
            });

            writer.WriteCommentValue("This is a comment at the top of the document.");

            // This "start object" token appears on the same line as the previous comment.
            writer.WriteStartObject();

            writer.WritePropertyName("ObjectProperty");
            writer.WriteCommentValue("This is a comment before an object value.");

            // This "start object" token appears on the same line as the previous comment.
            writer.WriteStartObject();

            writer.WritePropertyName("ChildProperty");
            writer.WriteStringValue("Property Value");
            writer.WriteEndObject();
            writer.WriteEndObject();

            writer.Flush();

            // Output to the console.
            output.Seek(0, SeekOrigin.Begin);
            using var streamReader = new StreamReader(output);
            Console.WriteLine(streamReader.ReadToEnd());
        }
    }
}
```

**.NET Fiddle:** [https://dotnetfiddle.net/PCSfnT](https://dotnetfiddle.net/PCSfnT)

#### 🧪 Expected Result

When writing a comment before writing an object, the comment should be on its own on a line.

```jsonc
/*This is a comment at the top of the document.*/
{
  "ObjectProperty": 
  /*This is a comment before an object value.*/
  {
    "ChildProperty": "Property Value"
  }
}
```

#### 🔎 Actual Result

When writing a comment before writing an object, the start object token is on the same line as the comment.

```jsonc
/*This is a comment at the top of the document.*/{
  "ObjectProperty": 
  /*This is a comment before an object value.*/{
    "ChildProperty": "Property Value"
  }
}
```

#### ⚖ Difference

Differences between the actual result (red) and the expected result (green).

```diff
- /*This is a comment at the top of the document.*/{
+ /*This is a comment at the top of the document.*/
+ {
    "ObjectProperty":
-   /*This is a comment before an object value.*/{
+   /*This is a comment before an object value.*/
+   {
      "ChildProperty": "Property Value"
    }
  }
```

### Adding newlines between the end of comments and the start of arrays

#### ✅ Test Case

```csharp
using System;
using System.IO;
using System.Text.Json;

namespace SystemTextJsonCommentNewlineProposal
{
    class Program
    {
        private static void Main(string[] args)
        {
            var output = new MemoryStream();
            var writer = new Utf8JsonWriter(output, new JsonWriterOptions()
            {
                // Important as proposed changes should only occur to indented Json only.
                Indented = true
            });

            writer.WriteCommentValue("This is a comment at the top of the document.");

            // This "start array" token appears on the same line as the previous comment.
            writer.WriteStartArray();

            writer.WriteCommentValue("This is a comment before an array element.");
            writer.WriteStartArray();
            writer.WriteCommentValue("This is a comment before an object in an array.");
            writer.WriteStartObject();
            writer.WritePropertyName("ChildProperty");
            writer.WriteCommentValue("This is a comment before an array value.");

            // This "start array" token appears on the same line as the previous comment.
            writer.WriteStartArray();

            writer.WriteStartObject();
            writer.WritePropertyName("ChildProperty");
            writer.WriteStringValue("Property Value");
            writer.WriteEndObject();
            writer.WriteEndArray();
            writer.WriteEndObject();
            writer.WriteEndArray();
            writer.WriteEndArray();

            writer.Flush();

            // Output to the console.
            output.Seek(0, SeekOrigin.Begin);
            using var streamReader = new StreamReader(output);
            Console.WriteLine(streamReader.ReadToEnd());
        }
    }
}
```

**.NET Fiddle:** [https://dotnetfiddle.net/8uskG7](https://dotnetfiddle.net/8uskG7)

#### 🧪 Expected Result

When writing a comment before writing an array, the comment should be on its own on a line.

```jsonc
/*This is a comment at the top of the document.*/
[
  /*This is a comment before an array element.*/
  [
    /*This is a comment before an object in an array.*/
    {
      "ChildProperty":
      /*This is a comment before an array value.*/
      [
        {
          "ChildProperty": "Property Value"
        }
      ]
    }
  ]
]
```

#### 🔎 Actual Result

When writing a comment before an array **at the start of the file**, the comment is on the same line as the beginning of the array.

Comments before array elements

```jsonc
/*This is a comment at the top of the document.*/[
  /*This is a comment before an array element.*/
  [
    /*This is a comment before an object in an array.*/
    {
      "ChildProperty":
      /*This is a comment before an array value.*/[
        {
          "ChildProperty": "Property Value"
        }
      ]
    }
  ]
]
```

#### ⚖ Difference

Differences between the actual result (red) and the expected result (green).

```diff
- /*This is a comment at the top of the document.*/[
+ /*This is a comment at the top of the document.*/
+ [
    /*This is a comment before an array element.*/
    [
      /*This is a comment before an object in an array.*/
      {
        "ChildProperty":
-       /*This is a comment before an array value.*/[
+       /*This is a comment before an array value.*/
+       [
          {
            "ChildProperty": "Property Value"
          }
        ]
      }
    ]
  ]
```

## ⚙ Configuration

**.NET Version:** 5.0.102

## ℹ Other information

*'The JSON5 Data Interchange Format'* Available at: <https://spec.json5.org/> [Accessed 6 May 2021]

